 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Short answer:** Women's job market in Victoria has loosened over the past year. Employment is still near record levels, but unemployment has risen from its late-2025 low and is now about where men's is.\n\n**Latest (September 2026, seasonally adjusted, Victorian women):**\n- **Unemployment rate: 5.1%.** It was 4.0% in October 2025 and 4.5% in July 2024. It peaked at 5.2% in July 2026 and has eased slightly since.\n- **Employed: about 1.84 million.** That's up roughly 77,000 from July 2024, though it dipped in March–April 2026 before recovering.\n- **Unemployed: about 99,000,** up from about 83,000 in July 2024.\n- **Participation rate: 63.6%,** up from 63.1% in July 2024. More women are in the labour force, so some of the rise in unemployment reflects more people looking for work.\n- **Employment-to-population ratio: 60.4%.** This is roughly flat over two years and below the 61.3% peak in September 2025.\n\n**Compared with men in Victoria (September 2026):**\n- Unemployment is 5.0%, almost identical to women's 5.1%. A year ago men's rate was higher than women's.\n- Men's participation has fallen to 71.5%, down from 73.3% in July 2024, while women's has risen.\n\n**Caveat:** These are monthly survey estimates and move around by a few tenths of a point from month to month. The direction over the past six to eight months is more reliable than any single reading.",
